WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:07.480
Welcome to the lesson! We already know the
notion of a vector and can perform simple

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:14.760
linear operations on vectors. Now let us look
at vectors in three-dimensional space.

3
00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:20.320
It is very convenient to work with vectors in
a rectangular coordinate system.

4
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:29.500
First, let us consider the rectangular
Cartesian coordinate system Oxyz in space.

5
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:36.380
We label the coordinate axes Ox, Oy and Oz and
also show

6
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:40.940
the unit vectors i, j and k, the basis
vectors.

7
00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:47.690
Take any point M in three-dimensional space.
We choose any vector a

8
00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:54.210
in space and place it at the starting point,
so that vector a

9
00:00:54.210 --> 00:01:00.670
equals vector OM. Let us find the projections
of vector a onto the coordinate axes.

10
00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:07.040
Through the end of vector OM we draw planes
parallel to the coordinate planes.

11
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:12.440
We label the points where these planes meet
the axes M₁, M₂

12
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:18.740
and M₃ respectively. By the rule for adding
vectors,

13
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:25.580
the vector a itself equals the sum of the
vectors OM₁, OM₂ and OM₃.

14
00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:32.480
The vectors obtained as products have the
following form:

15
00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.180
each of them equals the product of the
corresponding unit vector and the length

16
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.385
of the corresponding vector. If we denote the
projections

17
00:01:42.385 --> 00:01:45.040
of vector a onto the coordinate axes

18
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:51.740
by aₓ, a_y and a_z respectively, these
equalities give the following formula.

19
00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.390
This is called the expansion of the vector

20
00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:02.730
in terms of the unit vectors of the coordinate
axes. The numbers aₓ, a_y and a_z

21
00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.659
are called the coordinates of the vector,

22
00:02:05.659 --> 00:02:09.230
so the coordinates of a vector are its
projections

23
00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:12.547
onto the coordinate axes. In coordinate form,

24
00:02:12.547 --> 00:02:15.570
the vector equation is written like this.

25
00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:22.360
If we know the coordinates of a vector, it is
easy to find its length.

26
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.553
The length of a vector is the square root of
the

27
00:02:25.553 --> 00:02:28.680
sum of the squares of its coordinates, that
is,

28
00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.754
the magnitude of a vector equals the square
root of the

29
00:02:31.754 --> 00:02:34.660
sum of the squares of its projections onto the
axes.

30
00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:40.950
Knowing the coordinates of the start and end
of a vector, we can

31
00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:44.630
determine the coordinates of the vector
itself.

32
00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:48.570
We subtract the corresponding coordinates of

33
00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:52.330
its start from the coordinates of its end.

34
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.662
Let us look at an example. Two points are
given:

35
00:02:57.662 --> 00:03:00.720
A with coordinates five, six and minus

36
00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:07.060
eight, and point B with coordinates three,
five, nine.

37
00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:13.780
We need to find the length of vector AB. First
let us find the coordinates of AB.

38
00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:17.843
To do this, we subtract the corresponding
coordinates

39
00:03:17.843 --> 00:03:20.220
of A from the coordinates of B.

40
00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:26.360
As a result, we get the coordinates minus two,
minus one, seventeen.

41
00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:33.200
Now let us find the length: we square each
coordinate, add them up and take

42
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.320
the square root of the result.

43
00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:39.700
In our case we got the square root of two
hundred ninety-four,

44
00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.940
which equals seven times the square root of
six.

45
00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:51.160
Let us again consider vector a in a
rectangular coordinate system.

46
00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:58.150
We denote the angles between vector a and the
axes Ox, Oy and Oz by α,

47
00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:04.520
β and γ respectively. The cosines of these
angles are called direction cosines.

48
00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:11.520
The direction cosines of vector a are the
cosines of the angles α, β, γ

49
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:20.660
between vector a and the positive directions
of the axes Ox, Oy, Oz respectively.

50
00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:26.670
The direction cosines give the direction of
the vector. By the property

51
00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.850
of projecting a vector onto an axis,

52
00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:33.270
the projection of the vector equals the cosine
of its angle with the axis

53
00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:38.350
times the length of the vector.

54
00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.853
In other words, to find the direction cosines
of vector

55
00:04:41.853 --> 00:04:45.200
a, divide the corresponding coordinates of the
vector

56
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:51.480
by its magnitude. This gives a property of
direction cosines:

57
00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:56.880
the sum of the squares of the direction
cosines equals one.

58
00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:04.910
Let us look at an example. Let vector a have
coordinates four, minus six and three.

59
00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:08.596
Let us find the direction cosines of this
vector.

60
00:05:08.596 --> 00:05:11.380
First we find the length of vector a:

61
00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:16.030
it equals the square root of sixty-one.

62
00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:19.547
Now we substitute the coordinates of the
vector and

63
00:05:19.547 --> 00:05:22.650
its length into the direction cosine formula.

64
00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:26.710
We obtained the following results.

65
00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:33.900
Operations on vectors given in a rectangular
coordinate system are easier

66
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:39.880
than with their geometric picture. Later in
this lesson we will show how

67
00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:45.901
to add vectors with known coordinates and
multiply a vector by

68
00:05:45.901 --> 00:05:51.340
a number, and we will go through the examples
in detail.

69
00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:55.941
Let vectors a and b be given by their
projections

70
00:05:55.941 --> 00:05:58.770
onto the coordinate axes Ox, Oy, Oz,

71
00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:09.750
that is, their expansions in the unit vectors
of the axes are written in this form.

72
00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:17.610
Linear operations on vectors reduce to the
corresponding linear operations

73
00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:24.150
on their projections. For example, the sum or
difference of vectors can be written

74
00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:27.643
as the sum or difference of their coordinates.

75
00:06:27.643 --> 00:06:30.680
When a vector is multiplied by a scalar,

76
00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:35.020
its coordinates are also multiplied by that
scalar.

77
00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.573
Let us look at an example.

78
00:06:38.573 --> 00:06:43.020
Two vectors are given: vector a with
coordinates five, six

79
00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:49.720
and minus eight, and vector b with coordinates
three, five and nine.

80
00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:56.540
We need to find the vector c = 2a + b.

81
00:06:56.540 --> 00:07:02.960
First let us find the vector 2a: to do this,
we multiply the coordinates of vector a

82
00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:09.220
by two. We get a vector with coordinates ten,
twelve and minus sixteen.

83
00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:15.570
Now let us find the coordinates of vector c.
Vector c

84
00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:22.250
equals the sum of 2a and b; to find it, we add
the coordinates

85
00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:26.570
in the same positions. The calculation gives

86
00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:33.790
vector c with coordinates thirteen, seventeen
and minus seven.

87
00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:41.650
Using the coordinates of vectors, we can
determine whether vectors are equal.

88
00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:47.730
Vectors are called equal if their
corresponding coordinates are equal.

89
00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:49.870
Also, without drawing the vectors,

90
00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:54.150
we can check whether they are collinear using
the following formula:

91
00:07:54.150 --> 00:08:00.970
two vectors are collinear if the ratios of
their coordinates are equal.

92
00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:04.035
Vectors a with coordinates three,

93
00:08:04.035 --> 00:08:09.980
two and m, and b with coordinates nine, n and
twelve, are given.

94
00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:16.600
Find the values of the parameters n and m for
which these vectors are collinear.

95
00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:23.410
First, using the collinearity condition, we
set up a proportion

96
00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:25.772
of the coordinates of the vectors.

97
00:08:25.772 --> 00:08:29.870
This ratio gives two equations, from which we
find n and m.

98
00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:36.330
So we get the following result: when n equals
six and m

99
00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:41.690
equals four, vectors a and b are collinear.

100
00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:49.720
To sum up: vectors are one of the powerful
tools of mathematics and physics.

101
00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:53.447
The basic laws of mechanics and
electrodynamics are stated

102
00:08:53.447 --> 00:08:56.660
in the language of vectors. To understand
physics,

103
00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.540
you need to learn to work with vectors.

104
00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:04.057
In physics and mathematics, a vector is a
quantity

105
00:09:04.057 --> 00:09:07.090
described by a numerical value and a
direction.

106
00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:13.010
Many important quantities in physics are
vectors.

107
00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:18.690
For example, force, position, velocity,
acceleration, torque,

108
00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:22.470
momentum, and electric and magnetic fields.

109
00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:28.630
They can be compared with other quantities:
for example, mass, volume, pressure,

110
00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:33.032
temperature and density can be described by

111
00:09:33.032 --> 00:09:37.230
a single number; they are called scalars.
